Found it. The default is set in `APP/CODE/src/config/defaults.ts`:

```
export const DEFAULT_AGENT_ALLOWED_TOOLS = ['Read', 'Grep', 'Glob'] as const;
export const DEFAULT_AGENT_MAX_TURNS = 8;
export const DEFAULT_AGENT_PERMISSION_MODE = 'dontAsk' as const;
```

This flows into `loadConfig.ts:102`: `maxTurns: intFromEnv(ENV.agentMaxTurns, DEFAULT_AGENT_MAX_TURNS)`, and is consumed at the SDK boundary in `gateway/adapters/claude.ts:81`: `maxTurns: policy.maxTurns` (passed into the actual `query({ prompt, options })` call, per the comment at lines 7–10 confirming this matches the installed `@anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk` 0.3.220 `Options` type). It's also codified as a schema bound in `contracts/agentLoop.ts:24`: `maxTurns: z.number().int().min(1).max(64)`, with a Phase-0 fallback of `maxTurns: 1` (`DEFAULT_TOOL_POLICY`) used where tools are disabled — matching the historical note in `BUILD_LOG.md:763` (`SINGLE_ANSWER_MAX_TURNS`).

Since `.env.example:28` documents this explicitly as *"Dynamic agent loop (Claude-Desktop-style open-ended tools; not scripted workflows) ... Defaults: Read,Grep,Glob · maxTurns=8 · permissionMode=dontAsk"*, the implication for Claude Desktop parity is: Protean is deliberately running the same reason→tool→observe→continue loop substrate as Claude Desktop/Claude Code (multi-turn, `dontAsk` permissions, file-inspection tools) rather than a single-shot Q&A call — Bash/MCP parity is still withheld pending a proven sandbox (Phase 5).
